window.docContents[28].resolve({"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L1-___-Two-bodies/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L1-___-Two-bodies--Learning-path":{"contents":"* #* Do* Checked by HCP.L1_TwoBodies anchor* Concept disclosed\n* 1* Put oscillator B's mass in oscillator A's bare-real frequency radicand.* HCP.L1_TwoBodies#bare_real_cross_oscillators* The cross-oscillator expression elaborates as ℝ.\n* 2* Form the same spring-constant/mass quotient from kinded, bearer-free values.* HCP.L1_TwoBodies#kinded_bearer_free_cross_oscillators* The registered k/m edge accepts values from different oscillators.\n* 3* Declare oscillators A and B, give both the harmonic-oscillator sort, and compare their sorts.* HCP.L1_TwoBodies#oscillator_context_and_sort* Two particulars instantiate one sort.\n* 4* Give the oscillators distinct designations and prove a constant designation is not injective.* HCP.L1_TwoBodies#designation* A valid Designated instance cannot collapse distinct oscillators.\n* 5* Index mass and stiffness by one rig-scoped oscillator, form its k/m, and compare the mass catalogue entries.* HCP.L1_TwoBodies#oscillator_indexed_quantities* The accepted quotient reads the oscillator's fields; the dedicated mass kind is shared.\n* 6* Try cross-oscillator substitutions and addition; compare two weighings of oscillator A at different times on R₁.* HCP.L1_TwoBodies#refused_cross_oscillator; HCP.L1_TwoBodies#same_rig_estimate_difference* Cross-oscillator operations and cross-rig addition are refused; the same-rig time states admit a typed mass difference whose magnitude reads the real fields. Total mass is meaningful, but whose total requires a whole; that is L2.\n* 7* Ask for the mass kind dedicated to a nominal object with no sort.* HCP.L1_TwoBodies#refused_unsorted_object* A nominal Object cannot supply the Sorted instance required by dedicatedFor.\n\nWorksheet: HCP/Worksheets/L1_TwoBodies.lean · Solution: HCP/L1_TwoBodies.lean\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L1 — Two bodies","header":"5.2.6. Learning path","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L1-___-Two-bodies/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L1-___-Two-bodies--Learning-path"},"/Scale-spanning-units-___-a-third-unit-category-_LPAR_R13_RPAR_/A-criterion-the-dimension-layer-can-apply-___-and-one-place-it-cannot/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Scale-spanning-units-___-a-third-unit-category-_LPAR_R13_RPAR_--A-criterion-the-dimension-layer-can-apply-___-and-one-place-it-cannot":{"contents":"\n\nA dimension is mechanically reducible when it is expressible in mass, length, and time\nalone — it carries no charge and no temperature exponent. This is the\nFinkelstein–Whitehead test for \"not dimensionally independent of the four physical base\nunits\", specialized to the mechanical generators: such a unit needs no electromagnetic,\nthermal, luminous, or chemical base of its own.\n\nDimension.MechanicallyReducible d := d.charge = 0 ∧ d.temperature = 0. Power and energy\nare reducible (power_mechanicallyReducible, energy_mechanicallyReducible), as is\ndimension one; electric current is not (current_not_mechanicallyReducible — it carries\nthe electromagnetic generator), and neither is thermodynamic temperature in this work's\nalgebra (temperature_not_mechanicallyReducible).\n\nThe proposed three-way classification of SI units: physical base (the four\ndimensionally independent units kg, m, s, A), scale-spanning (the kelvin, candela,\nand mole), and derived (every plain product of powers, with no special coefficient).\nThe scale-spanning units are the new middle category\nthe two-way base/derived split omits.\n\ninductive UnitCategory | physicalBase | scaleSpanning | derived. Realized as a plain\nenumeration; a scale-spanning unit's category is scaleSpanning by construction.\n\nA scale-spanning unit pairs a metrological unit with its\nmechanical-dimension reduction and the human-selected coefficient that defines it. The\nworked set is the candela (reducing to power, via K_cd = 683 lm/W), the mole (to\ndimension one, via N_A), and the kelvin (to energy, via k_B). The coefficient and its\nvalue are citation locators — the published constants — not reproduced normative text.\n\nstructure ScaleSpanningUnit carries the unit, the reducesTo dimension, and the\ncoefficient symbol and value. The three canonical instances candela, mole, kelvin\nare declared in the ScaleSpanning namespace (so their names do not collide with the\nstandards parts that also declare them).\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Scale-spanning units — a third unit category (R13)","header":"12.1. A criterion the dimension layer can apply — and one place it cannot","id":"/Scale-spanning-units-___-a-third-unit-category-_LPAR_R13_RPAR_/A-criterion-the-dimension-layer-can-apply-___-and-one-place-it-cannot/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Scale-spanning-units-___-a-third-unit-category-_LPAR_R13_RPAR_--A-criterion-the-dimension-layer-can-apply-___-and-one-place-it-cannot"}});